WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.500
Django.

2
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.165
Sometimes you don't need a model server.

3
00:00:04.165 --> 00:00:08.067
You need a product around it, with users, forms and screens, fast.

4
00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:11.833
Django is the batteries included framework.

5
00:00:12.133 --> 00:00:17.267
It's for building products: dashboards, portals, internal tools with users and forms.

6
00:00:17.567 --> 00:00:22.300
You wouldn't serve an LLM through it, but you might build everything around the LLM with it.

7
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:24.833
Here's what's included.

8
00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:27.733
An ORM, where tables are just Python classes.

9
00:00:28.033 --> 00:00:31.133
Migrations, generated and versioned automatically.

10
00:00:31.433 --> 00:00:33.200
A full authentication system.

11
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.333
And the famous admin panel, a complete web UI over your models.

12
00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:40.433
Watch how one model fans out.

13
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:42.500
You write a single model class.

14
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.500
Django generates a versioned migration from it.

15
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.800
Running migrate creates the tables.

16
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.567
And registering the model gives you a working admin interface.

17
00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:53.733
Here's the code.

18
00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:59.600
This class defines the mission log table: a mission, a timestamp, a severity, and a note.

19
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.611
This one line registers it with the admin.

20
00:01:02.611 --> 00:01:05.000
Search, filters and edit forms, free.

21
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:09.900
Then three commands create the migration, build the tables, and start the server.

22
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.800
Two reasons teams like Django.

23
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:18.633
The admin alone can replace a week of hand built internal tool work, with permissions included.

24
00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:23.567
And convention over configuration keeps big teams consistent by default.

25
00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:26.000
Here's a real use.

26
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:32.000
Your evaluations need humans to grade five hundred answers as correct, partial or wrong.

27
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:39.433
Instead of building a labeling interface, you define one model, register it in the admin, and add two reviewer accounts.

28
00:01:39.733 --> 00:01:42.156
Reviewers get search and an edit form.

29
00:01:42.156 --> 00:01:44.833
You get graded data in Postgres by Friday.

30
00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:46.863
Let's recap.

31
00:01:46.863 --> 00:01:48.867
Django is batteries included.

32
00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:52.367
You get an ORM, migrations, auth and an admin.

33
00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:56.500
One model class fans out into a schema and a working interface.

34
00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.152
Use it to build the product around the LLM.

35
00:02:00.152 --> 00:02:01.633
Next up: REST APIs.
